    "intro": {
      "type": "fye-rich-text",
      "settings": {
        "band": "white",
        "variant": "default",
        "narrow": true,
        "eyebrow": "Diamond Education",
        "heading": "Four Words That Set the Price, and One That Sets the Sparkle",
        "body": "<p>Every diamond is described by the same four measures: cut, colour, clarity and carat. They are what a grading laboratory reports and what a price is built from.</p><p>They are not equally important. Cut decides how a diamond behaves in daylight, and it is the only one of the four that a cutter controls rather than nature. Colour and clarity mostly decide what you pay, not what you see, once you are past a certain point. Carat is the one everybody starts with and the one worth deciding last.</p><p>What follows is the order we would use ourselves.</p>"
      }
    },

    "fourcs": {
      "type": "fye-terms",
      "blocks": {
        "t1": {
          "type": "term",
          "settings": {
            "number": "01",
            "heading": "Cut, and it is not the shape",
            "body": "<p>Cut describes proportion and finish: how well the facets are placed to gather light and return it. It is the difference between a diamond that lifts across a room and one that looks flat under the same lamp.</p><p>Confusingly, cut is also used to mean shape, as in emerald cut. On a grading report, cut means quality of cutting. Aim for Excellent on a round brilliant, and accept nothing below Very Good.</p>",
            "meta": "Spend here first"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "term",
          "settings": {
            "number": "02",
            "heading": "Colour, which means absence of it",
            "body": "<p>White diamonds are graded D to Z, D being colourless and Z showing an obvious tint. The scale is finer than the eye is: most people cannot separate D from G with the stone in a ring.</p><p>Metal matters more than the letter. A G looks icy in platinum and warm in yellow gold, and a J that would look tinted in platinum looks perfectly white set in 18ct yellow.</p>",
            "meta": "Match to the metal"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "term",
          "settings": {
            "number": "03",
            "heading": "Clarity, at arm's length",
            "body": "<p>Clarity records the inclusions formed as the diamond grew. Grades run from Flawless down to I3, and almost all of that scale is invisible without a loupe.</p><p>What matters is whether you can see anything at normal distance, and whether an inclusion sits somewhere structurally unwise. VS2 and a well-placed SI1 are the value grades, and always worth looking at rather than buying by letter.</p>",
            "meta": "Eye clean is the target"
          }
        },
        "t4": {
          "type": "term",
          "settings": {
            "number": "04",
            "heading": "Carat, which is weight and not size",
            "body": "<p>A carat is a fifth of a gram. Two diamonds of the same weight can look quite different: a deep stone hides its weight in the bottom, a well proportioned one puts it across the top where you see it.</p><p>Prices step up sharply at the round weights, so 0.90ct often buys a visibly better diamond than 1.00ct for the same money, and nobody looking at the ring will know.</p>",
            "meta": "Buy just under the round figures"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": ["t1", "t2", "t3", "t4"],
      "settings": {
        "band": "ivory",
        "columns": "2",
        "numbering": "numbers",
        "narrow": false,
        "show_head": true,
        "eyebrow": "In Order of What You Will Notice",
        "heading": "The Four Cs, Ranked",
        "intro": "If you only take one thing from this page: cut is the one to protect, and clarity is the one people most often overpay for.",
        "note": ""
      }
    },

