        "eyebrow": "Cut",
        "heading": "What the Cut Grades Mean in Practice",
        "intro": "Cut grades are only reported on round brilliants. Fancy shapes such as oval, pear and emerald are graded for polish and symmetry instead, which is why proportions and a good look at the stone matter more on those.",
        "headers": "Grade | What you see | Worth buying",
        "first_col_bold": true,
        "col1_width": 20,
        "note": "On fancy shapes we look at length to width ratio, the shape of the outline, and whether a bow tie shadow runs across the middle. None of that appears as a single grade on a report.",
        "narrow": false
      }
    },

