    "faq": {
      "type": "fye-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "q": "Which of the Four Cs matters most?",
            "a": "<p>Cut, without much argument. It decides how the diamond behaves in light, and it is the only one of the four determined by the cutter rather than by how the stone formed. A modest stone cut beautifully looks better than a larger one cut poorly.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "q": "What does eye clean mean?",
            "a": "<p>That you cannot see any inclusions with the naked eye at normal viewing distance, roughly arm's length, without magnification. Many SI1 diamonds are eye clean and cost considerably less than VS grades. It is a description of the individual stone rather than a grade, which is why it needs looking at rather than reading.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "q": "Is a D colour diamond worth the premium?",
            "a": "<p>Only if the difference matters to you knowing it is there, because it will not be visible in the ring. Most people cannot distinguish D from G once a stone is set, and in yellow or rose gold the metal warms the appearance anyway. G and H give you white without the premium.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "q": "Why is 0.90ct so much cheaper than 1.00ct?",
            "a": "<p>Because prices step at the round weights, where demand concentrates. The visual difference between 0.90ct and 1.00ct is about a fifth of a millimetre across the top, which nobody can see. The price difference is substantial and can be spent on cut or colour instead.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "q": "Do the Four Cs apply to lab-grown diamonds?",
            "a": "<p>They have done, and largely still do in practice. In October 2025 GIA moved lab-grown reporting to a Premium or Standard framework rather than the traditional letters, but most lab-grown stones on the market still carry earlier GIA reports or reports from IGI and other laboratories using the familiar wording. We explain which framework a given report uses.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "q": "Do the Four Cs apply to coloured gemstones?",
            "a": "<p>Not in the same way. With a sapphire, ruby or emerald, colour is overwhelmingly the driver of both beauty and price, and inclusions are judged by type rather than on the diamond clarity scale. Emeralds in particular are almost always included and are graded on that expectation.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "q": "Should a small accent diamond have a report?",
            "a": "<p>No, and it would cost more than the stone. Reports are for centre stones and any stone of meaningful individual value. Accent and shoulder diamonds are specified by quality range instead, and we document that range with your order.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
